libstdc++: add an rtoskit-libc OS port (--with-rtoskit-libc)

libstdc++'s configure runs no link tests on a cross target. --with-newlib and
--with-picolibc hard-code what those libraries provide and use their ctype
tables; with neither, the generic cross configuration needs a linkable target.

--with-rtoskit-libc selects config/os/generic, which needs only the ISO
<ctype.h> functions, and defines what rtoskit-libc provides: the float math
functions and their long double forms, strtof, aligned_alloc, quick_exit and
at_quick_exit, thread-local storage, S_ISREG, <sys/types.h>, <inttypes.h>, <fcntl.h> and <unistd.h>.
The header probes compile as C in the compiler's default dialect, which before GCC 15 cannot
parse the C23-only <inttypes.h> or <fcntl.h>.
Names it lacks (memalign, link, setenv, sleep, ...) stay undefined.

The patch is the same for configure.ac and the generated configure, so no
autoconf run is needed. It applies to GCC 14, 15 and 16.

diff -ruN a/libstdc++-v3/configure b/libstdc++-v3/configure
--- a/libstdc++-v3/configure
+++ b/libstdc++-v3/configure
@@ -27525,7 +27525,125 @@
 
   # First, test for "known" system libraries.  We may be using newlib even
   # on a hosted environment.
-  if test "x${with_newlib}" = "xyes"; then
+  if test "x${with_rtoskit_libc}" = "xyes"; then
+    # rtoskit-libc: configure runs no link tests on a cross target, so say what the library provides, as the
+    # newlib and picolibc blocks do. The ISO <ctype.h> functions are all os/generic needs.
+    os_include_dir="os/generic"
+    $as_echo "#define HAVE_HYPOT 1" >>confdefs.h
+
+    $as_echo "#define HAVE_STRTOF 1" >>confdefs.h
+
+    $as_echo "#define HAVE_ACOSF 1" >>confdefs.h
